<UserControl x:Class="ConditioningControlPanel.Views.Controls.Studio.SchedulerRackPanel"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:loc="clr-namespace:ConditioningControlPanel.Localization"
             xmlns:feat="clr-namespace:ConditioningControlPanel.Features"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="488">

    <!-- ================================================================
         STUDIO RACK · SCHEDULER  (Phase 4)

         WHY THIS IS A HOST AND NOT A REBUILD
         The plan listed Scheduler/Ramp as a rescue from the dead
         ProgressionTabView copy (deleted in Phase 8). That copy was NOT the
         better one. Features/SchedulerFeatureControl.xaml has been live the whole
         time (reachable only through the obscure "Scheduler + Ramp" button
         on the Presets surface, MainWindow.Presets.cs:1021-1024), it owns
         its own load/save handlers, and the ProgressionTab twin is a
         strictly poorer subset with no handlers of its own - MainWindow
         drives it through LoadSettings/SaveSettings. Rebuilding the poorer
         copy here would have created a THIRD editor for the same nine
         settings. So this panel hosts the live control and adds only what
         the popup path never had: the help "?" that the dead ProgressionTab
         copy carried (HelpBtnScheduler, Tag="Scheduler") and a one-line
         statement of what the feature actually does.

         PHASE 8: the ProgressionTab twin is gone, and with it MainWindow's
         Load/SaveSettings round-trip through it. This panel's hosted control
         is now the only editor for all nine scheduler settings.

         Root MUST stay a Panel (StackPanel) and MUST stay the direct
         UserControl content: MainWindow.SessionFeatureLock.cs:365 inserts
         the session-lock banner with ((Panel)content.Content).Children
         .Insert(0, banner). The rack's detail host owns the scroller and
         the padding - do not add a ScrollViewer here or the banner insert
         loses its Panel.

         NOTHING on this panel is SessionLock.Owned, and that is deliberate
         and unchanged from the live control: the scheduler decides when a
         session begins, it prescribes no dose inside one.
         ================================================================ -->

    <StackPanel x:Name="StudioSchedulerPanel" x:FieldModifier="internal">

        <!-- What it does + the rescued help button. HelpContentService already
             ships a full "Scheduler" entry (Services/HelpContentService.cs:332);
             it was unreachable from the moment the ProgressionTab went dark
             until this button rescued it. -->
        <Border Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceBgBrush}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource GlassBorderBrush}"
                BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="10" Padding="16,12" Margin="0,0,0,10">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{loc:Str st4_scheduler_hint}"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}" FontSize="11"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                <Button Grid.Column="1" x:Name="HelpBtnStudioScheduler" x:FieldModifier="internal"
                        Style="{StaticResource HelpButtonStyle}" Tag="Scheduler"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"/>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- The live control, verbatim. Its own handlers write App.Settings
             and Save() on every edit (velvet-mosaic rule). -->
        <feat:SchedulerFeatureControl x:Name="SchedulerHost" x:FieldModifier="internal"/>

    </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
